<section class="hero"><div class="wrap dossier"><div class="eyebrow">Truth Trial · TW-CLAIM-000001 · Version 2</div><h1>Does God individually cause or permit every bad thing that happens?</h1><p>Trust-Worthy does not begin with “God has a reason for everything,” nor with “God has nothing to do with anything.” The first task is to test the hidden premise: must every harmful event be individually caused or specifically permitted by God?</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap dossier">
<div class="finding-box"><div class="eyebrow">Provisional finding</div><h2>Evidence favors rejecting the hidden premise as stated.</h2><p>The sources reviewed so far do <strong>not establish that every tragedy requires a separate act of divine causation or individualized permission</strong>. The New Testament contains language about hostile spiritual rule, ordinary physical events, human violence, and consequences that occur within the world. At the same time, other biblical traditions strongly affirm divine sovereignty, so the stronger claim “God is not in control of this world” also outruns the evidence.</p><p><strong>Most defensible present formulation:</strong> biblical texts portray God as sovereign while also portraying a world in which created agents, hostile powers, and ordinary causal processes have real operation. Exactly how those claims fit together remains a theological question rather than an established historical fact.</p></div>

<h2>1. The question contains an assumption</h2>
<div class="evidence"><strong>Assumption under test:</strong> If something happens, God must have either caused that specific event or consciously issued a specific permission for it.</div>
<p>That proposition is frequently assumed in popular theology, but it is not identical to the broader biblical claim that God is sovereign. “Sovereignty” can mean ultimate authority without logically entailing that every intermediate event is individually authored. Trust-Worthy therefore treats those as separate propositions.</p>

<h2>2. Jesus on tragedy: Luke 13</h2>
<p>Luke 13:1–5 places Jesus in front of two tragedies: people killed through Pilate's violence and eighteen people killed when a tower fell. Jesus rejects the inference that the victims suffered because they were worse sinners. The passage redirects the audience toward repentance rather than supplying a causal explanation for why those particular victims died.</p>
<div class="evidence"><strong>What this supports:</strong> tragedy cannot safely be reverse-engineered into a moral verdict about the victim. <strong>What it does not prove:</strong> the passage does not directly settle whether God permits, causes, foreknows, or merely allows natural and human causal chains to operate.</div>

<h2>3. Jesus speaks of a hostile “ruler of this world”</h2>
<p>John 12:31 calls a hostile figure “the ruler of this world” and says that ruler will be driven out. John 14:30 and 16:11 use closely related language. Within Johannine theology this is normally understood as Satan or the evil ruler opposing God.</p>
<p>This is significant because the text itself distinguishes the Father from a hostile ruler exercising real authority in the present world. It therefore complicates the simplistic proposition that every worldly event should be read as God's direct action.</p>
<div class="warning"><strong>Counterweight:</strong> this does not prove that the hostile ruler possesses ultimate sovereignty or that God lacks ultimate authority. The text portrays conflict and eventual judgment of that ruler.</div>

<h2>4. Paul: “the god of this age”</h2>
<p>Second Corinthians 4:4 contains the unusual expression “the god of this age,” traditionally understood as a hostile spiritual power who blinds minds. Scholarship debates details of the phrase and its relationship to the surrounding argument, so Trust-Worthy does not treat one verse as a complete cosmology. It nevertheless adds to the New Testament pattern in which hostile agency is real rather than illusory.</p>

<h2>5. Natural processes matter</h2>
<p>The philosophical problem of evil distinguishes moral evil from natural suffering. Free-will explanations may address human cruelty, but they do not by themselves explain earthquakes, disease, predation, genetic disorders, or other natural harms. One major theodicy argues that a stable world governed by regular causal laws necessarily makes harmful outcomes possible. Philosophers also raise serious objections: an omnipotent God could conceivably intervene selectively, alter initial conditions, or create different laws.</p>
<div class="evidence"><strong>Trust-Worthy inference:</strong> the existence of stable physical causation gives a coherent alternative to imagining every event as a separately issued divine decision. It does not, by itself, answer why an omnipotent God would choose this kind of world.</div>

<h2>6. The Gnostic Demiurge hypothesis</h2>
<p>Some ancient Christian movements offered a much more radical answer. The <em>Apocryphon of John</em>, preserved in multiple Coptic manuscripts, describes an ignorant lower ruler commonly identified as Yaldabaoth who fashions or rules the material cosmos beneath the transcendent divine reality. NASSCAL catalogs surviving witnesses including Nag Hammadi Codices II, III, IV and the Berlin Codex.</p>
<p>This demonstrates that early Christians did not all solve the problem of the world's suffering in the same way. Some distinguished the highest God sharply from the world's creator/ruler.</p>
<div class="warning"><strong>Important limit:</strong> the existence of the Demiurge tradition proves that this interpretation existed; it does <em>not</em> prove that the Demiurge actually exists. The surviving manuscripts are centuries later than the first Christian generation, while the underlying composition is generally placed earlier. The hypothesis must compete with other explanations rather than being smuggled in as fact.</div>

<h2>7. The strongest objection to our leading interpretation</h2>
<p>Classical Christian theology can answer that divine sovereignty and secondary causes are compatible: God may govern the whole without directly performing every action. On that model, human freedom, natural law, and hostile spiritual agency can all be real while remaining within providence. Furthermore, the problem of suffering becomes sharper—not weaker—if God has the power to prevent horrific suffering but chooses not to intervene.</p>
<p>This is the strongest challenge to simply saying “God doesn't let anything happen.” It changes the wording, but it does not remove the philosophical question of why a good and powerful God sustains a world containing apparently gratuitous suffering.</p>

<h2>8. Christ-consistency analysis</h2>
<p>The Gospel material reviewed here portrays Jesus responding to suffering by rejecting easy blame, healing where he can, opposing destructive powers, showing mercy, and calling people toward transformation. Luke 13 is particularly important: Jesus refuses to equate tragedy with greater guilt.</p>
<p><strong>Interpretive conclusion, not historical fact:</strong> a theology that casually tells sufferers “God did this to you for a reason” appears less consistent with this pattern than a theology that distinguishes God's character from the destructive event and responds with truth, mercy, healing, and responsibility.</p>

<h2>9. What remains unresolved</h2>
<ul class="rules"><li>Whether divine omniscience entails a meaningful form of permission.</li><li>How biblical sovereignty texts should be reconciled with hostile-ruler texts.</li><li>Whether spiritual powers described in ancient texts correspond to objective entities or theological symbolism.</li><li>Why a good, omnipotent God would create or sustain natural laws that allow extreme suffering.</li><li>Whether any proposed theodicy can adequately address horrendous, apparently gratuitous suffering.</li></ul>

<h2>10. What would change this finding</h2>
<p>A stronger case would need texts or historically early evidence explicitly teaching that every individual event is separately caused or specifically authorized by God, together with a coherent explanation of passages assigning meaningful agency to humans, hostile powers, and ordinary causes. Conversely, evidence that biblical sovereignty language does not mean universal providence would strengthen the opposite position.</p>

